PRE-REGISTRATION -- cross-BOX reproducibility of the deterministic configuration   2026-09-12
Written at 12:12, BEFORE any run on zc-01. Prompted by the user opening zc-01/zc-02
("those three boxes all use the same zakpod1").

WHY THIS MATTERS
  ADR-0157's claim is single-box: with ZAKCODE_TEMPERATURE=0 and
  ZAKCODE_STABLE_PROMPT_IDENTITY=1, runs on THIS box reproduce byte-for-byte.
  A user promised "reproducible runs" will expect the same bytes on a DIFFERENT
  machine against the same model. Nothing has measured that.

WHAT IS KNOWN BEFORE THE RUN
  - zc-01 platform.platform() == this box's, exactly:
    Linux-6.8.0-139-generic-x86_64-with-glibc2.39. The Environment block's OS
    line is therefore identical across the two boxes. (Had it differed, byte
    identity would be impossible by construction and this would be a one-line
    finding instead of an experiment.)
  - Same pod (zakpod1, 10.0.0.250:9090), same model, same source: the bench tree
    is rsync'd from this checkout (src identical to main 7ed0b90, the merged fix).
  - Pinned workspace path is a constant (/tmp/zbench-pinned-02-median-bug) on
    both boxes; the fix removes the session id from the prompt.
  - KNOWN THREAT, named now: tool OUTPUTS reach the model, and the task's pytest
    output carries a wall-clock duration ("10 passed in 0.01s"). ADR-0152 found
    that string varying within one box at higher iteration counts. Across boxes
    with different CPUs (20 vs this box's), the timing string is a plausible
    cross-box divergence source that is the TASK's, not the engine's.

CELLS on zc-01, 02-median-bug, N=3 each, ZAKCODE_TEMPERATURE=0
  X1  pin-both (bench instrument: constant workspace + constant session id)
  X3  real-user cell WITH the fix (workspace pinned, fresh uuid4, stable id ON)
  The local reference digests for the same two cells are recorded below BEFORE
  the zc-01 runs.

PREDICTIONS
  X1 zc-01 reproduces within itself: 3/3 byte-identical.
  X2 zc-01's X1 digest EQUALS this box's pin-both digest (7bff3055...): the
     deterministic configuration is reproducible ACROSS machines with matching
     platform strings. Predicted to HOLD. If it fails, the first diverging
     provider call must be diffed (ZBENCH_DUMP_REQUESTS + _diffcalls.py) before
     any attribution; the pytest timing line is the expected culprit, and if it
     IS the culprit the limit is the task's tool output, not the loop.
  X3 the fix cell reproduces within zc-01: 3/3 identical (the shipped claim,
     on a second machine) -- and its digest equals this box's fix-cell digest.

DECISION RULES
  X1 fails -> zc-01 + pod is not deterministic even with everything pinned;
     stop, diff the dumps, no cross-box claim.
  X1 holds, X2 holds, X3 holds -> ADR-0157 addendum: "reproducible across
     machines sharing the platform string, on this task". Scoped exactly so.
  X1 holds, X2 fails -> within-box yes, cross-box no. Diff the dumps; if the
     divergence is a tool-output timing string, record that cross-machine
     reproducibility is bounded by nondeterministic TOOL OUTPUT (a real-world
     limit any agent has) and consider whether the bench should normalise it.
  Anything else -> report the cell table and claim nothing beyond it.

CO-TENANCY: runs during the TOPK eval (short task, ~6 calls per run). Accepted
  in advance as latency-only, per the load control in ADR-0157's addendum.
